'''Julio Nakayama''' (born January 2, 1996), known by his in-game handles '''Naka''' and formerly '''Naka245''', is a Balboan professional [[esports]] player widely regarded as one of the greatest ''[[Galactic Combat]]'' players of all time. With five [[Galactic Combat World Championship]] (GCWC) titles across two different organisations, Nakayama is among the most decorated competitors in the game's history. He currently plays for and captains [[Banc Balboa Istmo Jocs]], a team he founded in 2024 after departing [[Força (esports)|Força]].

== Early life ==

Julio Nakayama was born on January 2, 1996, in [[Sant Bart]], Jala, [[Balboa]], and grew up in the city's Zona 6 neighbourhood. His father was a [[Nakamizu|Nakamian]] national and his mother was a third-generation [[Nakamian Balboan people|Nakamian Balboan]]. Following his parents' divorce in 2000, Nakayama split his childhood between Sant Bart, where he lived with his mother, and [[Aoyama]], [[Nakamizu]], where his father resided. The frequent travel between the two countries exposed him to both Balboan and Nakamian gaming communities.

Nakayama turned to video games as a coping mechanism following his parents' separation, spending much of his free time playing online. He developed a reputation in online lobbies for his strategic thinking and mechanical skill, rising through ranked play to become one of the highest-rated players in the Balboan region.

== Career ==

=== Correcamins (2015–2022) ===

When ''[[Galactic Combat]]'' was released, Nakayama quickly established himself as a top-ranked player. In 2015, he and fellow Balboan player Aleix Riera ("Tempesta") formed a team with three other players — Nil Masdeu ("Volt"), Ryo Sato ("Frost"), and Carles Pons ("Echo") — to compete in the inaugural [[Galactic Combat World Championship|GCWC]]. The team was named [[Correcamins (esports)|Correcamins]], after a popular Balboan video game from the 2000s.

Correcamins won the first GCWC tournament in 2015, establishing themselves as the dominant team of the early competitive era. Under Nakayama's in-game leadership, the team won additional GCWC titles in 2016, 2018, and 2020, making them the most successful organisation in the first decade of ''Galactic Combat'' competitive play. Nakayama's strategic shotcalling and versatile hero pool were frequently cited by analysts as the foundation of Correcamins' success.

=== Força (2022–2024) ===

In 2022, Nakayama and Riera were recruited to join the newly formed superteam [[Força (esports)|Força]], backed by [[Abyala Telecom]] and [[BalboaVisió]]. The move brought together two of Correcamins' star players into a roster of top talent assembled from across multiple competitive regions. In their first year, Força won the [[2023 Galactic Combat World Championship|2023 GCWC]], adding a fifth world title to Nakayama's career.

Following the 2023 championship, tensions between Nakayama and Riera intensified. The conflict centred on opposing competitive philosophies: Riera, the highest-rated player on the roster, favoured an aggressive, carry-oriented strategy built around his individual playmaking, while Nakayama advocated for a more coordinated, team-based approach. The situation was exacerbated by the organisation's heavy promotion of Riera as the public face of the team, which Nakayama felt marginalised other players. A series of strategy disputes during the [[Balboa Galactic Combat Cup|BGCC]] Winter 2024 playoff matches precipitated the final break, and Nakayama departed the organisation in early 2024, citing "irreconcilable differences in competitive philosophy."

=== Banc Balboa Istmo Jocs (2024–present) ===

After leaving Força, Nakayama secured sponsorship from [[Banc Balboa]], one of the largest banks in Balboa, and founded Istmo Esports S.A. as the operating company. The new team, [[Banc Balboa Istmo Jocs]], was assembled with a mix of Balboan talent and international players from [[Okaiken]], [[Nakamizu]], and [[Burawa]].

In their first competitive season, Banc Balboa Istmo Jocs finished fourth in the BGCC Summer 2024 split and were eliminated in the quarterfinals. In 2025, the team improved significantly, finishing as runners-up in the Winter split before winning the Summer split, upsetting Força in the playoff final. The victory qualified them for the [[2025 Galactic Combat World Championship]], where they reached the semifinals. In 2026, Banc Balboa Istmo Jocs defended their Summer BGCC title, winning back-to-back Summer splits, and qualified for the [[2026 Galactic Combat World Championship]] in [[Okami]], [[Okaiken]].

The Nakayama–Riera rivalry became one of the most discussed storylines in ''Galactic Combat'' competitive coverage, with Nakayama's Summer 2025 BGCC victory over Força widely characterised as vindication for his team-oriented philosophy.

== Personal life ==

Nakayama is married to Maria Lim, the daughter of [[Lim Hui-jin]], the current [[President of Balboa]]. The marriage has drawn additional media attention to Nakayama's public profile, linking the esports star to the country's first family.

